                "reply_count": 13,
                "retweet_count": 3,
                "source": "Twitter Web App",
                "text": "Medicaid (called Medi-Cal here in California) is supposed to be a safety net for children, people with disabilities, and low-income families. The new budget reconciliation law strengthens that system by making sure it\u2019s focused on those who truly rely on it.\n\nHere\u2019s what\u2019s changing. Able-bodied adults without young kids who choose not to work will now be expected to work, volunteer, or pursue education at least 80 hours a month to keep getting benefits. It\u2019s a commonsense, bipartisan idea that promotes stability and leads to better health outcomes.\n\nAt the same time, the law tightens eligibility checks and steps up verification of citizenship and immigration status to help crack down on waste, fraud, and abuse. Over the last decade, improper Medicaid payments have totaled more than half a trillion dollars nationwide, with $56 billion just last year. That\u2019s unacceptable.\n\nFor states like California that have pushed Medicaid far beyond its original scope, the law gradually scales back certain federal matching rates, meaning states will pick up a fairer share of the costs. It also closes loopholes that let states draw down extra federal money without actually improving care.\n\nAs for concerns about rural healthcare, the law provides $10 billion a year for five years, totaling $50 billion, to help keep small rural hospitals and clinics open. How that money gets distributed in California will come down to the Governor and state officials. It\u2019s critical that Sacramento gets this right so our small towns and rural residents aren\u2019t left behind.\n\nMore details on changes to Medicaid can be found at the link below: https://t.co/FTEQhIC8Fu",
                "text_lang": "en",
